  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 112 PM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nRadar shows light showers across the higher terrain at this hour.\nToday will continue our streak of above-average days with high \ntemperatures in the 80s for the plains. SW flow aloft has \nincreased over the last 24 hours. Thus, winds could be breezy in \nour mountains and the eastern plains this afternoon. With relative\nhumidity dropping into the teens across the eastern plains, we \nexpect a few hours of elevated to near-critical fire weather \nconditions for those locations this afternoon. Some isolated to \nscattered showers will continue to remain possible in the higher \nterrain today, with a low (~10-20%) chance for these to push off \ninto the urban corridor and the adjacent plains. Little measurable\nrainfall is expected and this would only be widely scattered in \nthe higher terrain. Some showers could continue through the \novernight for far western portions of our area including Jackson, \nGrand, and Summit counties. Given increasing high-level cloud \ncover overnight, it will be another unseasonably warm night with \nanother record warm minimum for the day possible. 57 degrees \n(1881) is the record warm minimum for Sunday.\n\nOn Sunday, the remnants of Tropical Storm Rachel move into the \nwestern US with any notable precipitation staying well to the west \nof our forecast area. For our area, high temperatures should take a \nslight dip, but will remain in the 80s for most of the plains. \nAnother round of isolated to scattered showers will be possible for \nthe high country, mainly west of the Continental Divide. The \nforecast starts to become more uncertain by Monday. It is becoming \nmore likely that a backdoor cold front will stall somewhere \nover/just north our northeastern counties. South of the front, \ntemperatures should stay in the upper 70s to near 80, but north of \nthe front, temperatures could drop into the low 70s. Regardless, we \nexpect this front to push through the area by Tuesday morning \nbringing a \"cool\" down with highs into the upper 60s to low 70s on \nthe plains (except for our far southeastern counties) for Tuesday \nafternoon- still a few degrees above average for this time of year.\n\nThere are still significant differences in the forecast across the \nlatest model suite starting Tuesday night through the end of the \nweek. The position/speed/strength of the trough and upper-level \nlow(s) remains uncertain. The ECMWF has trended slower and further \nwest over the last few runs while the GFS moves a somewhat weaker \nlow over or north of our area. In general, we expect PoPs to \nincrease starting late Tuesday and peak on Wednesday with Thursday \ncontinuing to trend drier. Note that a vast majority of ensemble \nmembers (~90%) give Denver some measurable rainfall, with amounts \ngenerally under 1/2 inch. Some of the moisture associated with the \nremnants of Hurricane Simon could be pulled into the flow aloft, \nmainly affecting areas to our east. As snow levels drop through the \nweek and moisture increases, the mountains and high country (mainly \nabove 10,000 feet) could see a few inches of snow. Our northern \nmountains in particular look most favorable.\n\nFrom Thursday onward, the GFS progresses the upper-level low off to \nthe east with another low moving from Canada into the Great Lakes \nregion towards the end of the week and into the weekend. The ECMWF \nkeeps the upper-level low hung up over the Four Corners through the \nend of the week. Regardless, high temperatures look to finally stay \nnear to below average through at least next Saturday with at least \nsome low PoPs in the mix.",
